AB  - In recent years, diffusion model-based recommender systems have significantly improved recommendation performance. Due to their excellent structural denoising mechanisms, they also exhibit strong robustness against traditional data poisoning attacks. Existing attack methods mainly rely on fake user injection. Because the fake nodes and discrete edges constructed by these methods lack inherent logical consistency, they are typically identified as abnormal data and smoothed out by the diffusion process. To break through this defense, we propose an anti-denoising profile pollution attack named RMGAE. Unlike traditional fake user injection, RMGAE aims to covertly bypass the structural denoising mechanism of diffusion models by implanting a small number of logically consistent adversarial interaction sequences into the historical records of real users. First, we utilize semantic associations in the knowledge graph to construct intermediate bridging items, thereby enhancing the logical connectivity between target and popular item clusters. Subsequently, we model the construction of adversarial interaction sequences as a bilevel combinatorial optimization problem and design a collaborative search strategy that integrates reinforcement learning (RL) and an evolutionary algorithm (EA). This strategy uses an RL module to dynamically capture gradient feedback from the target model, precisely guiding the EA to bypass denoising filtration even within a massive discrete search space. By adaptively searching for adversarial interaction sequences that conform to normal user behavior distributions while maintaining high attack efficacy, the proposed method ultimately constructs highly covert polluted profiles. Extensive experiments on three real-world datasets demonstrate that RMGAE outperforms 12 existing baselines.
